I am in favor of stabilizing this as implemented here but I would flag one unfortunate behavior not mentioned in the stabilization report. According to @petrochenkov in https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/70420#issuecomment-604643571:

> With the scheme from my comment above `$expr.$float` won't work because `$float` is not a float token, it's an "interpolated" token, it basically have invisible parentheses around it.

`$expr.$float` not working is the desired behavior (PR should add a test), but while it may be the case for rustc's parser, syn and proc macros in general have no way to make the same distinction. In the proc macro below, `$expr.$float` appears indistinguishable from `tuple.0.0` in the input token stream and the proc macro can only accept both or neither.

```rust
// lib.rs

// [dependencies]
// syn = { version = "1.0", features = ["full", "extra-traits"] }

use proc_macro::TokenStream;
use syn::{parse_macro_input, Expr};

#[proc_macro]
pub fn repro(input: TokenStream) -> TokenStream {
    let expr = parse_macro_input!(input as Expr);
    println!("{:#?}", expr);
    TokenStream::new()
}
```

```rust
// main.rs

macro_rules! m {
    ($expr:expr, $float:tt) => {
        repro!($expr.$float);
    };
}

m!(tuple, 0.0);

fn main() {}
```

