A mutable reference is indeed created here for `IndexMut`, I think. However, `slice` is "derived from" `x` through `raw_pointer`, so this is not unlike
```rust
let x = &mut [...];
let slice = &mut *x;
```
which also has "two aliasing mutable references", but one is derived from the other *and they are not used in interleaved ways*, so we are good. The following would be bad, but is prevented by the borrow checker:
```rust
let x = &mut [...];
let slice = &mut *x;
slice[1] = 2;
x[1] = 3; // invalidates slice
slice[1] = 2; // bad because slice is invalid!
```

The moment `x` gets used/reborrowed again, `slice` becomes invalid (its "reborrow ends", except there is no borrow checker checking that). This is confirmed by Miri which [only errors on the last line here](https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=stable&mode=debug&edition=2018&gist=df45a276a0ca7e244ed2045fe3322e2e), so there was no error before.

