This is not the most efficient way to move the rejected substrings if there are a lot of matches. For example, consider `"1a2a3a4".to_string().remove_matches("a")`:
```
The sequence of intermediate strings is:
1a2a3a4 (the initial string)
12a3a4 (copy 2a3a4)
123a4 (copy 3a4)
1234 (copy 4)
```
The amount of copying work can grow quadratically with the input length, since a substring after the `n`th match will be moved `n` times.
A better solution is to keep a cursor to the end of the copied data and then to move each unmatched substring once:
```rust
let mut last_match_end = 0;
let mut written_end = 0;
for (start, end) in matches {
    // This copy can be skipped if the slice to copy is empty
    // or last_match_end == written_end
    self.vec.copy_within(last_match_end..start, written_end);
    last_match_end = end;
    written_end += start - last_match_end;
}
// This copy can be skipped if the slice to copy is empty
// or last_match_end == written_end
self.vec.copy_within(last_match_end.., written_end);
written_end += self.len() - last_match_end;
```
The code would be a bit simpler if you used `searcher.next_reject()` instead of `searcher.next_match()`, as those are the substrings that need to be copied.

