Your code is equivalent to:

```rust
use std::mem;

pub fn take_first_mut<'a, T>(slice: &mut &'a mut [T]) -> Option<&'a mut T> {
    let (first, rem) = mem::take(slice).split_first_mut()?;
    *slice = rem;
    Some(first)
}
```

Just adding a `'a` to the outer reference makes it compile without `mem::take`:

```rust
pub fn take_first_mut<'a, T>(slice: &'a mut &'a mut [T]) -> Option<&'a mut T> {
    let (first, rem) = slice.split_first_mut()?;
    *slice = rem;
    Some(first)
}
```

IIUC in this case `&mut &'a mut Self` is equivalent to `&'a mut &'a mut Self` because of how lifetime elision works. So (again, IIUC) adding the `'a` to the outer reference should not change the API of the function at all.

>IIUC in this case `&mut &'a mut Self` is equivalent to `&'a mut &'a mut Self` because of how lifetime elision works.

If the two were equivalent, changing it from one to the other wouldn't have made non-compiling code compile 🙂

It's essential here that the return value of these methods borrows from the "inner" slice, which could have a very long lifetime, and not from the "outer" reference to it, which could be much more short-lived. Changing the type of `slice` to `&'a mut &'a mut [T]` would needlessly restrict the lifetime of the return value to the outer lifetime of the input value.

