If the LHS capacity were larger then it might be that someone did a

```rust
let v = Vec::with_capacity(...);
v.append(other);
```

to preallocated, e.g. because `append` called in a loop. It would be surprising if then a smaller vector would get swapped in, negating the preallocation.

One scenario where the swap approach would be counter-productive is the following:

```rust
let mut lhs = Vec::new();
let mut rhs = Vec::with_capacity(1000_0000);

rhs.resize_with(10, Default::default);
lhs.append(&mut rhs); // A
rhs.resize_with(1000_0000, Default::default); // B

// ... do additional work with rhs
```

Step A would now move the allocation to lhs and thus B would incur a second big allocation which wouldn't have happened without this change.

I think I can avoid this issue by only performing the swap when the moved allocation matches Vec's growth strategy, i.e. when it is not excessively large. This should work out as an improvement because all we do is trade a potential allocation _of similar size_ in the future to avoid a certain allocation now.
With the current code the issue is that the allocation in the future could be much larger than the allocation we're saving now.

