I was hoping to avoid scattering coverage-specific exceptions in unrelated parts of the codebase. (If the code changes, it's easy to miss little unrelated piece of logic like this.) It would be nicer if each optimization could be turned on or off via session option, rather than having them grouped together under these hardcoded numbers.

There is a way to still get the advantages of centralized and accessible optimizations by level, but also allows optimizations to be turned on or off granularly, during command line processing:

In `rustc_session` add a new field to the `Options` struct (using the `bitflags!` pattern currently applied to `SanitizerSet`):

```rust
    mir_opts: MirOpt,
...

bitflags! {
    #[derive(Default, Encodable, Decodable)]
    pub struct MirOpt: u16 {
        const CONST_PROP_BASIC = 1 << 0;
        const RETURN_PLACE    = 1 << 1;

        const MIR_OPT_LEVEL_1 = (1 << 2) - 1;

        const CONST_PROP_COPIES_AND_MOVES  = 1 << 2;
        const DEST_PROP  = 1 << 3;
        const EARLY_OTHERWISE  = 1 << 4;
        const INLINE  = 1 << 5;
        const MATCH_BRANCH  = 1 << 6;

        const MIR_OPT_LEVEL_2 = (1 << 7) - 1;

        const CONST_IDENTITY  = 1 << 7;
        const RETURN_TERM  = 1 << 8;
        const UNREACHABLE_PROP  = 1 << 9;

        const MIR_OPT_LEVEL_3 = (1 << 10) - 1;
    }
}
```

and then, at the top of `build_session_options()`, enable each option based on the selected mir-opt-level:

```rust
    if debugging_opts.mir_opt_level >= 1 {
        mir_opts.insert(MirOpt::MIR_OPT_LEVEL_1);
    }
    if debugging_opts.mir_opt_level >= 2 {
        mir_opts.insert(MirOpt::MIR_OPT_LEVEL_2);
    }
    if debugging_opts.mir_opt_level >= 3 {
        mir_opts.insert(MirOpt::MIR_OPT_LEVEL_3);
    }
```

