We discussed this stabilization in the libs team meeting earlier today and are on board with stabilizing this behavior as a macro with a suitable name.

We felt vaguely positive about the name `pend!` and there were no objections or other popular contenders, so those of us present feel prepared to stabilize as `core::task::pend!`, but want to open an opportunity for public discussion as well, since this name has not been raised in the thread so far.

## Rationale

This is a macro. **The core thing that this macro does** (the reason it needs to be a macro in the first place) is a control flow operation of early-returning `Poll::Pending` if necessary. The action verb "pend" characterizes informing the caller that pending is taking place.

As a verb that pertains to the behavior of this operation *as a macro*, as opposed to anything that a function would be capable of doing, we don't expect that this name would clash with the name of any functions -- unlike `ready!` (https://doc.rust-lang.org/stable/std/future/fn.ready.html) or `pending!` (https://doc.rust-lang.org/stable/std/future/fn.pending.html).

Neither `ready!` nor `pending!`, as adjectives, characterize the early-return nature of this macro.

None of the other suggestions such as `try_ready!` or `try_resolve!` or `try_await!` or `resolve!` or `complete!` characterize what this macro does to "make a thing ready"/"resolved"/"complete", which is that all it does is inform the caller that pending is taking place, until such time as it no longer is.

## Example

The existing doc example, rewritten to use `pend!`:

```rust
use std::task::{pend, Context, Poll};
use std::future::{self, Future};
use std::pin::Pin;

pub fn do_poll(cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<()> {
    let mut fut = future::ready(42);
    let fut = Pin::new(&mut fut);

    let num = pend!(fut.poll(cx));
    // ... use num

    Poll::Ready(())
}
```

