This is incorrect and doesn't catch cases where e.g. the layout-constrained field is deeper:

```rs
#[rustc_layout_scalar_valid_range_start(1)]
struct NonZero<T>(T);

let mut foo = unsafe { NonZero((1,)) };
let a = &mut foo.0.0;
```

In fact, a place can contain all of these kinds of expressions: https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/d34a3a401b4e44f289a4d5bf53da83367cbb6aa7/compiler/rustc_mir_build/src/build/expr/category.rs#L38-L44

So as long as we see these, we're still in the same place and we need to continue looking for layout-constrained fields.

Now it's too restrictive:

```rs
let foo = unsafe { NonZero((1,)) };
&mut { foo.0 }.0;
```

This compiles under MIR unsafeck because the block doesn't belong to the place and creates a new value by copying the content `(1,)`, which doesn't belong to `foo` anymore. To fix this I'd expect you just need to also stop on any expression that's not in `Category::Place` (the list above).

I'd expect the same logic for assignments as for borrows, i.e. look for layout-constrained fields inside the place we assign to. Here's an example:

```rs
let mut foo = unsafe { NonZero((1,)) };
foo.0.0 = 0;
```

